{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"fernlike-toothsome-hen","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe MCP client URL that every doc and the committed example instruct clients to use —\n`https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp/` (WITH the trailing slash) — answers with a 307\nredirect to the **http://** scheme. The redirect target is plaintext, and no HSTS header is\npresent on the response to upgrade it back.\n\n## Evidence (unauthenticated reads; no credential was sent)\n\n    $ curl -s -o /dev/null -D - https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp/\n    HTTP/2 307\n    location: http://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp\n\n    $ curl -s -o /dev/null -D - -X POST -d '{}' https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp\n    HTTP/2 401\n    www-authenticate: Bearer error=\"invalid_token\", error_description=\"Authentication required\",\n      resource_metadata=\"https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp\"\n\nNeither response carried a `strict-transport-security` header.\n\n## Why this matters\n\nA 307 PRESERVES the method and body, and clients differ in whether they retain an\n`Authorization` header across a same-host scheme change. A client that retains it transmits the\nper-client bearer PAT over plaintext. Even a client that drops the header still sends the request\nbody in the clear. Without HSTS there is no transport-layer backstop.\n\nThe exposure is widened by the documentation pointing AT the affected form:\n\n- `examples/mcp-clients/codex/config.toml:18` — `url = \".../mcp/\"`\n- `docs/mcp-client-setup.md:54,73,96`\n- `docs/mcp-auth.md:252` — the OAuth `auth_resource_server_url` audience\n\n`docs/mcp-auth.md:258` already records the asymmetry deliberately: 'The external edge is `/mcp/`\nbut the app serves `http_path = \"/mcp\"`'. The redirect implementing that mapping is what emits\nthe http:// Location.\n\n## How it was found\n\nWhile debugging `affable-bleakish-yellowlegs` (Codex omitting the rebar MCP server). The live\n`~/.codex/config.toml` had drifted to the NO-slash form `/mcp`, so a discriminating experiment\nwas run to decide whether the URL or the missing PAT caused the omission. The URL was ELIMINATED\nas that bug's cause — `/mcp` returns a clean 401 — but the probe surfaced this. Note the\nuncomfortable implication: the drifted local config was on the SAFER URL than the documented one.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `https://<box>/mcp/` no longer redirects to an `http://` target — the redirect (if kept)\n      preserves the `https` scheme, or the edge serves the path directly.\n- [ ] An HSTS header is served, so a client cannot be walked down to plaintext.\n- [ ] A test or probe fails if the edge ever emits a non-https `Location` for the MCP paths.\n- [ ] Documented/example client URLs are confirmed to reach the authenticated endpoint without a\n      scheme downgrade.\n\n## Security note\n\nReported from unauthenticated probes only; no PAT was transmitted during this investigation and\nno credential value appears in this ticket.","id":"79b2-6ebc-6c1d-4125","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","auth","security","nginx","edge"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Documented MCP client URL /mcp/ 307-redirects to plaintext http:// with no HSTS"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787856439265243001,"uuid":"70d8ce1a-d96e-410e-8c7c-c9e67e227689"}